1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,588
Your app is slow.

2
00:00:01,588 --> 00:00:03,124
Your app shows an error.

3
00:00:03,124 --> 00:00:04,596
Your app lost an order.

4
00:00:04,596 --> 00:00:06,004
Nine times out of ten,

5
00:00:06,004 --> 00:00:08,628
the answer is in one place: the database.

6
00:00:09,178 --> 00:00:11,108
Today you meet post gress Q L,

7
00:00:11,108 --> 00:00:12,909
the database most teams run.

8
00:00:12,909 --> 00:00:14,068
You connect to it,

9
00:00:14,068 --> 00:00:15,097
you look inside,

10
00:00:15,097 --> 00:00:17,349
and you run your first transaction.

11
00:00:17,899 --> 00:00:22,805
Not slides. A real database, a real terminal, and a lab that checks your work.

12
00:00:23,355 --> 00:00:24,571
Meet the database.

13
00:00:26,121 --> 00:00:29,193
Here is what to expect, from the first minute to the last.

14
00:00:29,743 --> 00:00:35,226
One: the words you need. Database, schema, table, connection, transaction, migration.

15
00:00:35,776 --> 00:00:41,941
Two: a live demo. One real post gress server. We connect with P S Q L, and look around.

16
00:00:42,491 --> 00:00:46,139
Three: we ask questions with sequel, and check who is connected.

17
00:00:46,689 --> 00:00:49,307
Four: we run a transaction that rolls back,

18
00:00:49,307 --> 00:00:50,585
and one that commits.

19
00:00:50,585 --> 00:00:51,498
Then your turn,

20
00:00:51,498 --> 00:00:54,177
with a lab that judges you with five checks.

21
00:00:54,727 --> 00:00:59,442
This is lesson one of the databases domain. Everything that follows builds on it.

22
00:00:59,992 --> 00:01:01,208
Meet the database.

23
00:01:03,258 --> 00:01:06,586
Eight words. Learn them once, and database talk gets easy.

24
00:01:07,136 --> 00:01:09,440
A server runs the database software.

25
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:13,152
A database is one named collection of data on that server.

26
00:01:13,702 --> 00:01:16,416
A schema is a folder inside the database.

27
00:01:16,416 --> 00:01:18,932
A table is a grid of rows and columns,

28
00:01:18,932 --> 00:01:21,382
like a spreadsheet with strict rules.

29
00:01:21,932 --> 00:01:27,095
A row is one record, like one customer. A column is one property, like a name.

30
00:01:27,645 --> 00:01:29,404
A connection is how your app,

31
00:01:29,404 --> 00:01:29,829
or you,

32
00:01:29,829 --> 00:01:30,981
talk to the server.

33
00:01:30,981 --> 00:01:32,255
Each one uses memory,

34
00:01:32,255 --> 00:01:33,469
so they are limited.

35
00:01:34,019 --> 00:01:37,571
A transaction is a group of changes that succeed together,

36
00:01:37,571 --> 00:01:38,612
or fail together.

37
00:01:38,612 --> 00:01:39,715
Nothing half done.

38
00:01:40,265 --> 00:01:43,434
A migration is a versioned change to the structure,

39
00:01:43,434 --> 00:01:44,366
kept in a file,

40
00:01:44,366 --> 00:01:48,094
so every environment gets the same change in the same order.

41
00:01:49,644 --> 00:01:54,849
Go to the lab folder.

42
00:01:56,399 --> 00:01:58,298
Look around. Type: L S.

43
00:01:58,848 --> 00:02:04,032
A data folder, and a server log. A post gress server is already running in the background.

44
00:02:04,582 --> 00:02:09,382
Connect with psql.

45
00:02:09,932 --> 00:02:11,486
The prompt now says shop,

46
00:02:11,486 --> 00:02:11,797
hash.

47
00:02:11,797 --> 00:02:13,537
You are inside the database,

48
00:02:13,537 --> 00:02:14,283
called shop.

49
00:02:14,283 --> 00:02:16,396
Everything you type now is sequel,

50
00:02:16,396 --> 00:02:17,825
or a backslash command.

51
00:02:18,375 --> 00:02:29,618
Who and where am I?

52
00:02:30,168 --> 00:02:35,459
Database shop, user student. A sequel statement always ends with a semicolon.

53
00:02:36,009 --> 00:02:41,961
Which version?

54
00:02:42,511 --> 00:02:46,777
Version sixteen. Write that number down, it is one of your lab answers.

55
00:02:47,327 --> 00:02:49,994
List the tables.

56
00:02:50,544 --> 00:02:51,342
Four tables,

57
00:02:51,342 --> 00:02:53,203
in the schema called public.

58
00:02:53,203 --> 00:02:53,868
Customers,

59
00:02:53,868 --> 00:02:54,333
orders,

60
00:02:54,333 --> 00:02:54,931
products,

61
00:02:54,931 --> 00:02:57,125
and one called schema migrations.

62
00:02:57,125 --> 00:02:59,717
Count them: that is another lab answer.

63
00:03:00,267 --> 00:03:05,515
Describe a table.

64
00:03:06,065 --> 00:03:08,406
Each column has a name and a type.

65
00:03:08,406 --> 00:03:09,782
Version is a number,

66
00:03:09,782 --> 00:03:11,572
and it is the primary key,

67
00:03:11,572 --> 00:03:13,500
so no two rows can share it.

68
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:14,945
Applied on is a date,

69
00:03:14,945 --> 00:03:16,391
filled in by default.

70
00:03:16,941 --> 00:03:24,599
Read the table.

71
00:03:25,149 --> 00:03:25,679
One row.

72
00:03:25,679 --> 00:03:26,472
Version one,

73
00:03:26,472 --> 00:03:28,060
and when it was applied.

74
00:03:28,060 --> 00:03:32,360
This is how a database remembers which migrations it already has.

75
00:03:32,910 --> 00:03:40,974
Count the orders.

76
00:03:41,524 --> 00:03:44,212
Twelve orders. That is your second lab answer.

77
00:03:44,762 --> 00:03:50,991
Read the customers.

78
00:03:51,541 --> 00:03:56,981
Five customers, each with an I D. That I D is how other tables point at a customer.

79
00:03:57,531 --> 00:04:23,857
Join three tables.

80
00:04:24,407 --> 00:04:25,187
Five orders,

81
00:04:25,187 --> 00:04:27,008
each with the customer name,

82
00:04:27,008 --> 00:04:27,788
the product,

83
00:04:27,788 --> 00:04:28,893
and the quantity.

84
00:04:28,893 --> 00:04:29,739
Three tables,

85
00:04:29,739 --> 00:04:30,584
one question.

86
00:04:30,584 --> 00:04:32,535
That is what relational means.

87
00:04:33,085 --> 00:04:51,389
Who is connected?

88
00:04:51,939 --> 00:04:57,059
One connection: ours. Later, you will use this view to find leaks and stuck sessions.

89
00:04:57,609 --> 00:05:20,393
Start a transaction.

90
00:05:20,943 --> 00:05:21,896
Six customers.

91
00:05:21,896 --> 00:05:26,595
But notice the star in the prompt: we are inside an open transaction.

92
00:05:26,595 --> 00:05:28,025
Nothing is final yet.

93
00:05:28,575 --> 00:05:39,071
Roll back.

94
00:05:39,621 --> 00:05:44,549
Five again. Ghost never existed. A rollback undoes everything since begin.

95
00:05:45,099 --> 00:06:08,459
Commit a transaction.

96
00:06:09,009 --> 00:06:09,730
Zed stays.

97
00:06:09,730 --> 00:06:11,675
But look at his I D: seven.

98
00:06:11,675 --> 00:06:13,260
Six was used by Ghost.

99
00:06:13,260 --> 00:06:15,133
A rollback undoes the row,

100
00:06:15,133 --> 00:06:16,214
not the number.

101
00:06:16,214 --> 00:06:17,223
Remember that.

102
00:06:17,773 --> 00:06:20,610
Leave psql. Type: backslash, Q.

103
00:06:21,160 --> 00:06:30,909
Write the three answers.

104
00:06:31,459 --> 00:06:34,339
Read it back. Type: cat, answers dot text.

105
00:06:34,889 --> 00:06:37,535
Four tables, twelve orders, version sixteen.

106
00:06:38,085 --> 00:06:40,453
Run the judge.

107
00:06:41,003 --> 00:06:41,716
Five checks.

108
00:06:41,716 --> 00:06:42,786
You counted right,

109
00:06:42,786 --> 00:06:45,283
and the transactions did what they should.

110
00:06:45,283 --> 00:06:46,293
Five out of five.

111
00:06:46,843 --> 00:06:48,806
Clean up.

112
00:06:49,356 --> 00:06:52,492
The server is stopped, and the data folder is removed.

113
00:06:53,042 --> 00:07:02,301
Is the server really gone?

114
00:07:02,851 --> 00:07:05,304
No response. Nothing is left running.

115
00:07:06,854 --> 00:07:11,889
Your task. Connect, look around, answer three questions, and run two transactions.

116
00:07:12,439 --> 00:07:16,001
One: lab up, then P G, to connect to the shop database.

117
00:07:16,551 --> 00:07:21,053
Two: list the tables, and count them. Count the orders. Read the server version.

118
00:07:21,603 --> 00:07:24,632
Three: write the three numbers into answers dot text.

119
00:07:25,182 --> 00:07:28,034
Four: insert Ghost inside a transaction,

120
00:07:28,034 --> 00:07:29,032
and roll back.

121
00:07:29,032 --> 00:07:30,173
Then insert Zed,

122
00:07:30,173 --> 00:07:30,958
and commit.

123
00:07:30,958 --> 00:07:32,098
Five: lab check,

124
00:07:32,098 --> 00:07:33,097
then lab down.

125
00:07:35,147 --> 00:07:38,923
Here is where to find everything. Pause on this screen, and write it down.

126
00:07:39,473 --> 00:07:41,121
The lab lives on GitHub,

127
00:07:41,121 --> 00:07:42,288
at it dash waves,

128
00:07:42,288 --> 00:07:43,868
slash dev ops dash A I,

129
00:07:43,868 --> 00:07:45,378
in the folder academy,

130
00:07:45,378 --> 00:07:46,065
slash lab.

131
00:07:46,615 --> 00:07:52,354
Two files: lab one twenty-six dot S H, and shortcuts one twenty-six dot S H.

132
00:07:52,904 --> 00:07:53,808
Install it,

133
00:07:53,808 --> 00:07:56,684
on any Linux machine: sudo install,

134
00:07:56,684 --> 00:07:58,574
dash m seven five five,

135
00:07:58,574 --> 00:08:00,792
lab one twenty-six dot S H,

136
00:08:00,792 --> 00:08:02,025
into slash usr,

137
00:08:02,025 --> 00:08:03,011
slash local,

138
00:08:03,011 --> 00:08:03,997
slash S bin.

139
00:08:04,547 --> 00:08:09,425
Load the short names with: source shortcuts one twenty-six dot S H.

140
00:08:09,425 --> 00:08:10,663
You get two: lab,

141
00:08:10,663 --> 00:08:11,246
and P G.

142
00:08:11,796 --> 00:08:13,452
You need post gress Q L,

143
00:08:13,452 --> 00:08:14,694
server and client,

144
00:08:14,694 --> 00:08:15,384
installed,

145
00:08:15,384 --> 00:08:17,799
and port fifty-four sixty-one free.

146
00:08:17,799 --> 00:08:20,559
The lab runs its own private post gress,

147
00:08:20,559 --> 00:08:21,801
in its own folder.

148
00:08:22,351 --> 00:08:24,058
Lab up prepares everything.

149
00:08:24,058 --> 00:08:25,765
Lab check judges your work.

150
00:08:25,765 --> 00:08:27,282
Lab down removes it all.

151
00:08:27,282 --> 00:08:29,306
Only run it on your own machine.

152
00:08:31,356 --> 00:08:33,425
Pass or fail. You need all five.

153
00:08:33,975 --> 00:08:35,810
One: you counted the four tables.

154
00:08:36,360 --> 00:08:38,301
Two: you counted the twelve orders.

155
00:08:38,851 --> 00:08:40,729
Three: you read the server version.

156
00:08:41,279 --> 00:08:43,625
Four: the committed transaction kept Zed.

157
00:08:44,175 --> 00:08:46,991
Five: Ghost is gone, and it used up an I D.

158
00:08:47,541 --> 00:08:48,843
To pass, all five.

159
00:08:50,893 --> 00:08:52,664
Your turn, on your own machine.

160
00:08:53,214 --> 00:08:55,454
One: lab up, and connect with P G.

161
00:08:56,004 --> 00:08:59,289
Two: list the tables, count the orders, and read the version.

162
00:08:59,839 --> 00:09:02,868
Three: write your three answers into answers dot text.

163
00:09:03,418 --> 00:09:08,090
Four: run the two transactions, Ghost with a rollback, then Zed with a commit.

164
00:09:08,640 --> 00:09:12,459
Five: lab check until you see five out of five. Then lab down.

165
00:09:13,009 --> 00:09:14,844
Pause the video, and begin now.

166
00:09:30,394 --> 00:09:31,269
Quick check.

167
00:09:31,819 --> 00:09:35,275
What is the difference between a database, a schema and a table?

168
00:09:35,825 --> 00:09:40,283
Why does a sequel statement end with a semicolon, and a backslash command not?

169
00:09:40,833 --> 00:09:43,585
What does a rollback undo, and what does it not undo?

170
00:09:44,135 --> 00:09:45,970
Which view tells you who is connected?

171
00:09:46,520 --> 00:09:49,011
Objectives covered: M C fourteen,

172
00:09:49,011 --> 00:09:50,747
objectives one and two.

173
00:09:50,747 --> 00:09:51,880
The vocabulary,

174
00:09:51,880 --> 00:09:53,691
connecting with P S Q L,

175
00:09:53,691 --> 00:09:55,352
and diagnostic sequel.

176
00:09:55,902 --> 00:09:57,971
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

177
00:10:08,521 --> 00:10:12,937
Next: who is allowed to do what. Users, passwords, and least privilege.

